                                                "sense_id": "adf8b754-f7d8-3835-b2b0-7522cc7aa5d8",
                                                "text": "Seven pounds make a clove, 2 cloves a stone, 2 stone a tod, 6+¹⁄₂ tods a wey, 2 weys a sack, 12 sacks a last. The 'Pathway' points out the etymology of the word cloves; it calls them ' claves or nails.' It is to be observed here that a sack is 13 tods, and a tod 28 pounds, so that the sack is 364 pounds."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0c6d74fb-03a5-4c0b-af97-098802403ea2",
                                                "sense_id": "adf8b754-f7d8-3835-b2b0-7522cc7aa5d8",
                                                "text": "By a statute of 9 Hen. VI. it was ordained that the wey of cheese should contain 32 cloves of 7 lbs. each, i.e. 224 lbs., or 2 cwts."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
